# Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

Successfully implemented Vercel Web Analytics for this Django project.

## Changes Made

### Modified Files:
- `templates/layout/base_layout.html` - Added Vercel Analytics script tag

## Implementation Details

Since this is a Django project (not a JavaScript framework), I followed the HTML script tag approach as documented in the official Vercel Analytics documentation:

1. **Fetched Latest Documentation**: Retrieved the current implementation instructions from:
   - https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart
   - https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/package

2. **Identified Framework**: Confirmed this is a Django project which requires the HTML script tag method rather than the npm package `@vercel/analytics`.

3. **Added Analytics Script**: Added the following script tag to the `<head>` section of `templates/layout/base_layout.html`:
   ```html
   <!-- Vercel Web Analytics -->
   <script defer src="https://cdn.vercel-insights.com/v1/script.js"></script>
   ```

4. **Verified Coverage**: Confirmed that 19 templates extend `base_layout.html`, ensuring analytics will be tracked across all pages of the application including:
   - Home page
   - User accounts (login, register, profile, edit profile)
   - Delivery dashboard and orders
   - Food items (display, detail)
   - Restaurant pages
   - Orders (cart, checkout, history)
   - And more

## Why This Approach

- **Framework Compatibility**: Django is a Python framework, so the JavaScript npm package `@vercel/analytics` is not applicable.
- **Official Documentation**: The Vercel docs specifically mention the script tag approach for HTML-based implementations.
- **CDN Delivery**: Using `https://cdn.vercel-insights.com/v1/script.js` ensures the analytics script is always up-to-date and cached efficiently.
- **Defer Loading**: The `defer` attribute ensures the script doesn't block page rendering.

## Next Steps

Once this code is deployed to Vercel:

1. Enable Web Analytics in your Vercel project dashboard
2. Navigate to the Analytics tab in your project
3. The script will automatically begin collecting page view data
4. You can verify the implementation by checking the browser's Network tab for requests to `/_vercel/insights/*`

## Notes

- No package.json modifications were needed (this is a Python/Django project)
- No build dependencies were added
- The implementation is minimal and non-invasive
- All existing functionality is preserved
- The script loads asynchronously and won't affect page performance
